Total energy produced by the reactor in time $t=10 \mathrm{yr}$.
$\begin{aligned}
E & =1000 \times 10^6 \times 10 \times 3.15 \times 10^7 \mathrm{ J} \\
& =3.15 \times 10^{17} \mathrm{ J}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & \because \quad \text{ Efficiency }=\frac{\text{ output energy }}{\text{ input energy }} \\ & \text{ Input energy caused by fission }=\frac{\text{ output energy }}{\text{ efficiency }}\end{aligned}=\frac{3.15 \times 10^{17}}{(10 / 100)}=3.15 \times 10^{18} \mathrm{ J}$
Energy produced by one fission of ${ }^{235} \mathrm{U}=200 \mathrm{MeV}\begin{aligned}
& =200 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-13} \mathrm{ J} \\
& =3.2 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{ J}
\end{aligned}$
Therefore, number of fissions required
$=\frac{\text{ total energy }}{\text{ energy per fission }}=\frac{3.15 \times 10^{18}}{3.2 \times 10^{-11}}=9.8 \times 10^{28}$
Hence, mass of uranium required is given by
$\begin{aligned}
m & =\frac{N}{N_a} \times 235=\frac{9.8 \times 10^{28}}{6.02 \times 10^{26}} \\
& =38.2 \times 10^3 \mathrm{ kg}
\end{aligned}$

By using $N=N_0\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{1 / 1 / 2}\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & t=\frac{T_{12} \log _e\left(\frac{N_0}{N}\right)}{\log _e(2)} \\
\Rightarrow & t \propto \log _e \frac{N_0}{N} \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{t_1}{t_2}=\frac{\log _e\left(\frac{N_0}{N}\right)_1}{\log _e\left(\frac{N_0}{N}\right)_2}
\end{array} \\ Hence, $\quad $\frac{6}{10}=\frac{\log _e(8 / 1)}{\log _e\left(N_0 / N\right)}\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow& \log _e\frac{N_0}{N}=\frac{10}{6} \log _ e(8)=\log _e 32 \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{N_0}{N}=32
\end{array}<br/>So, fraction that decays $=1-$\frac{1}{32}=\frac{31}{32}$

The number of counts left after time $t\begin{array}{ll} & N=N_0\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{t / T_{1 / 2}} \\
\therefore & 30=240\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{60 / T_{1 / 2}} \\
\text{ or } & \frac{30}{240}=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{60 / T_{1 / 2}}[\because t=1 \mathrm{ h}=60 \mathrm{ min}] \\
\text{ or } & \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{60 / T_{1 / 2}}
\end{array}$
Comparing the powers, we get $\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore& \frac{60}{T_{1 / 2}}=3 \\ \text{ and } T_{1 / 2}=\frac{60}{3}\\
\text{ or }& T_{1 / 2}=20 \mathrm{ min}
\end{array}$

Remaing amount $N=N_0\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n\begin{aligned}
N & =6.4 \times 10^{10}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12 / 4} \\
& =0.8 \times 10^{10} \text{ atoms }
\end{aligned}$

Let initial amount be $N_0$.
Remaining amount $N=N_0-\frac{7 N_0}{8}=\frac{N_0}{8}\therefore \quad N=N_0\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & \frac{N_0}{8}=N_0\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n \\ \Rightarrow & \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n \\ \Rightarrow & n=3\end{array}
\therefore \quad \frac{t}{T_{1 / 2}}=3\begin{gathered}\Rightarrow \quad\frac{t}{15}=3 \text{ or }$ t=15 $\times3 \\ t=45 \mathrm{ h}\end{gathered}$

